WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.708
[lively music]

00:00:02.833 --> 00:00:05.083
This is an exhibit
celebrating the 400th

00:00:05.083 --> 00:00:08.583
anniversary of the First Folio,
which is the first edition of

00:00:08.583 --> 00:00:12.000
the collected plays of
Shakespeare, published in 1623.

00:00:12.166 --> 00:00:14.416 align:start position:0% line:0%
One reason why we
care about this book

00:00:14.416 --> 00:00:16.750 align:start position:0% line:0%
and celebrate its anniversary
is that it preserves

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:20.000 size:84% position:42% line:0%
about half of Shakespeare's
plays that otherwise might

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.833 align:start position:0% line:0%
have been lost, because they
don't appear in other

00:00:22.125 --> 00:00:23.458
print editions from the period,

00:00:23.458 --> 00:00:26.458
so plays like Macbeth
and The Tempest that we think of

00:00:26.458 --> 00:00:29.500
as being absolutely essential
to the Shakespeare canon.

00:00:29.666 --> 00:00:33.541
But I think another reason
why we celebrate this book is a

00:00:33.541 --> 00:00:35.375
a bit more complicated and
connected to

00:00:35.375 --> 00:00:38.083
Shakespeare's cultural
status, because the book

00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:41.000
itself is not actually that rare
for an early modern book.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.000
There's over 200 copies of it,

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:46.000
so its value seems less
connected to its rarity

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.416
or scarcity and more
connected to

00:00:48.416 --> 00:00:50.958
the values and meanings
that we have brought to it

00:00:50.958 --> 00:00:52.333
over the centuries.

00:00:52.333 --> 00:00:53.375
I would be very happy

00:00:53.375 --> 00:00:55.750
if people came to the
exhibit who didn't know

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.166
what the First Folio was.

00:00:57.208 --> 00:00:59.958
To me, the really fascinating
story is how it became

00:00:59.958 --> 00:01:01.416
so important, and in
the exhibit,

00:01:01.416 --> 00:01:03.000
I was interested
in exploring that

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.541
intersection of turning
Shakespeare into a

00:01:05.541 --> 00:01:07.833
commodity that could be
worth quite a lot.

